<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/10th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Riade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/933">933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Riade was fought in March 933, probably near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unstrut">Unstrut</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, between King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> raiding army. Henry had used a nine-year truce to build fortifications and train armoured cavalry, and when he refused further tribute the Magyars invaded and were routed. The victory raised the prestige of the Saxon dynasty, although Magyar raids continued until Otto I's victory at the Lechfeld in 955.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-riade.jpg" alt="Battle of Riade" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinrich_I._k%C3%A4mpft_gegen_die_Ungarn.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> had served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> under Emperor Arnulf, and after his death in 899 they campaigned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>, breaking up Great Moravia in 906 and destroying a Bavarian army under Margrave Luitpold at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pressburg">Battle of Pressburg</a> a year later. In 924 a Magyar army invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> defeated Henry in the field, but the capture of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a> prince, probably Zoltán, near Pfalz Werla allowed him to negotiate, and a truce with annual tribute was declared in 926.</p><p>Henry used the truce to reorganise the defences of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and subdue the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Polabian Slavs</a>. A 926 assembly approved new castles and a new garrison duty: soldiers were grouped in nines of agrarii milites, farmer-soldiers, one on guard while the other eight worked the fields, and all nine manned the castles in an invasion. In 932, with the support of the church at the Synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>, Henry stopped paying the tribute; he allegedly had a dead dog thrown down before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> envoys.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Henry levied mounted contingents from every stem duchy, though only the chronicler Flodoard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> records the Bavarian presence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a> calls the Thuringian contingent inermes, probably meaning poorly armed, and says Henry could not fully trust his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> horsemen. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>, who had besieged an unknown town, tried to withdraw by night when Henry camped nearby at Riade. He sent ahead a screen of foot soldiers with a few horsemen, then met the Magyar light cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> with lightly armoured troops followed by a massed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> attack. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> the Magyars fled at the approach of Henry's horsemen, and the victorious troops proclaimed Henry emperor on the battlefield.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The exact site is unknown. Several places in Central Germany claim it, among them Kalbsrieth at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unstrut">Unstrut</a> and Helme and the Hunnenfeld near Riethgen, and the Riade named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> probably denotes Henry's camp rather than the battlefield. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> invaded Germany again in 954, during a rebellion instigated by Duke Liudolf of Swabia, and were finally defeated by Henry's son and successor Otto I at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Battle of Lechfeld</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Riade” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Henry the Fowler, as king, prepared for the Magyar threat by negotiating a nine-year truce in 926, building fortifications and training cavalry, and defeated the Magyars at the <b>Battle of Riade</b> in 933.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Military</a></li></ul></section>
